---
/**
 * The site footer.
 *
 * EXTRACTED from Contact.astro, which rendered it inline — fine while
 * the site was one page, wrong the moment a legal page needed the same
 * footer. Two copies of a footer carrying CC BY-SA credits is two
 * places for the attribution to rot, and the licence is breached the
 * moment one of them stops rendering.
 *
 * It stays inside Contact's <section> on the homepage (so the dark band
 * and the top rule read as one block) and is rendered directly by the
 * legal pages. getContent() memoizes its promise, so fetching here
 * rather than taking props costs nothing.
 *
 * IT CARRIES ITS OWN DARK BAND, and must.
 *
 * Every colour in here is a /NN opacity of `primary-foreground` —
 * white — because the footer was written INSIDE Contact's
 * `bg-primary-deep text-primary-foreground` section and silently
 * inherited both. Rendered bare on a legal page it kept the white text
 * and lost the dark ground: white on warm paper, about 1.1:1, a footer
 * that was fully present in the DOM and invisible on screen.
 *
 * So the band belongs to the footer, not to whatever wraps it.
 * `bg-primary-deep` is idempotent on the homepage — Contact's section
 * is already that exact colour, so this repaints the same value and
 * nothing moves.
 */
import Logo from './Logo.astro';
import { getContent } from '../content';
const { site, imageCredits, legalPages } = await getContent();
---

<footer class="relative bg-primary-deep text-primary-foreground border-t border-primary-foreground/15">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 py-10">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-6">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4">
        <Logo class="w-[3.3rem]" />
        <div>
          <p class="decal text-xs">Namaste Dandeli</p>
          <p lang="kn" class="mt-0.5 text-sm text-primary-foreground/55">
            ನಮಸ್ತೆ ದಾಂಡೇಲಿ
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-primary-foreground/45">
        &copy; {new Date().getFullYear()} {site.legalName}
      </p>
    </div>

    {imageCredits.length > 0 && (
      <details class="mt-8 text-xs text-primary-foreground/45">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer hover:text-primary-foreground/70">
          Photograph credits
        </summary>
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-1.5">
          {imageCredits.map((c) => (
            <li>{c}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </details>
    )}

    {/*
      The bottom bar: legal links on the left, the build credit on the
      right.

      THE BAR IS UNCONDITIONAL; THE LINKS INSIDE IT ARE NOT.

      The legal array is empty until a page has a body, and an empty
      array renders no links at all. That absence IS the gate: there is
      never a link to a page that does not exist, which is the one
      failure this whole site was built to fix. verify-content asserts
      that the link set and the built page set match exactly.

      But the credit is not gated on anything, so it cannot live inside
      that conditional — unpublishing a policy would silently take it
      down with the links. Hence the bar renders either way and only
      the <nav> is conditional. `sm:ml-auto` holds the credit right
      whether or not the links are beside it.

      THE BAR STACKS BELOW sm, AND MUST. Side by side at 320px the two
      legal links take nearly the whole line and leave the credit a
      sliver, so "Built by" stayed up on the links' line and the
      avatar + "Kunal" dropped beneath it — a broken-looking orphan.
      flex-wrap cannot fix that: the credit is ONE flex item, so
      wrapping moves all of it or none, and at that width neither the
      links nor the credit will yield. So the bar is a column on
      phones and only becomes a row at sm, where both fit.

      Array order is the footer order, set by dragging in the Studio —
      see siteSettings.legalPages.

      `aria-label` on a <nav> is fine: <nav> has an implicit navigation
      role. The guardrail forbids it on ROLELESS generics (div, span),
      where the browser discards it.
    */}
    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-col items-start gap-y-4 border-t border-primary-foreground/10 pt-6 sm:flex-row sm:flex-wrap sm:items-center sm:justify-between sm:gap-x-6 sm:gap-y-3">
      {legalPages.length > 0 && (
        <nav aria-label="Legal">
          <ul class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-2 text-xs text-primary-foreground/45">
            {legalPages.map((p) => (
              <li>
                <a
                  href={`/legal/${p.slug}`}
                  class="link-inline underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-primary-foreground/70 hover:no-underline"
                >
                  {p.title}
                </a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </nav>
      )}

      {/*
        The build credit, matching kumarsrivathsan.com and
        keepingitsou.com — same wording, same placement, same link.

        `alt=""` on the avatar is deliberate: the anchor already says
        "Kunal" in text and carries an aria-label, so a described
        avatar would announce the name twice. It is decoration beside a
        label, which is exactly what empty alt is for.

        IT IS A flex-nowrap ROW, NOT AN INLINE PHRASE, so that
        "Built by (avatar) Kunal" holds one line as the sibling sites
        render it.

        `whitespace-nowrap` ALONE DOES NOT WORK HERE and looks like it
        should. global.css sets `p { text-wrap: pretty }`, and
        text-wrap writes the same longhand group as white-space, so on
        a <p> it resets text-wrap-mode and the nowrap is silently
        dropped — the class is right there in the markup while the
        computed style reads `normal`. Hence `.nowrap-phrase`
        (global.css, defined directly beneath that `p` rule), which
        sets both longhands, AND the flex row, which keeps the label
        and link as unbreakable siblings even if that rule ever
        changes. `shrink-0` on both stops the avatar being squeezed.

        It is a NAMED class, not `[text-wrap:nowrap]`. An arbitrary
        variant exists only if Tailwind's scanner saw that exact
        string, and a stale dev-server scan served CSS without it
        while the production build had it — the credit was correct in
        `dist/` and broken in `pnpm dev`, so it looked fixed from
        whichever side you were not looking at.

        The image is hotlinked from GitHub rather than re-hosted, which
        is the one exception to the Sanity-CDN rule in CLAUDE.md. It is
        a deliberate choice for parity with the sibling sites — see the
        note there before "fixing" it.
      */}
      <p class="nowrap-phrase flex flex-nowrap items-center gap-1 text-xs text-primary-foreground/55 sm:ml-auto">
        <span class="shrink-0">Built by</span>
        <a
          href="https://kunalkeshan.dev"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          aria-label="Built by Kunal"
          class="link-inline inline-flex shrink-0 items-center gap-1 whitespace-nowrap text-primary-foreground/70 underline underline-offset-4 transition-colors hover:text-primary-foreground hover:no-underline"
        >
          <img
            src="https://github.com/kunalkeshan.png"
            width="16"
            height="16"
            alt=""
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            class="size-4 shrink-0 rounded-full"
          />
          Kunal
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</footer>
